  'throttle-point': { title: 'GPU throttle temperature', value: '≈85 °C', why: 'NVIDIA does not publish a junction throttle point for these data-center GPUs; ≈85 °C is the commonly used ballpark for silicon of this class, shown only to place the die’s working temperature relative to it.' },
  'package-stiffener-drawing': { title: 'GPU package stiffener ring and substrate surface', value: 'one flat ring and clustered capacitors; sizes and placement representative', why: 'Large lidless flip-chip packages commonly bond a flat metal stiffener ring to the substrate top to limit warpage, leaving the laminate edge exposed. No public drawing gives this package’s ring width, thickness or corner radii, so the ring is drawn as a typical one, not measured from a product. No public capacitor map was found either: the clusters of two capacitor sizes, their count and positions, and the solder-mask pattern are illustrative.' },
  'hbm-stack-drawing': { title: 'HBM stacks, as drawn', value: 'height about 3x real; layer bands, top print and spacer representative', why: 'A real HBM stack is a thin molded block whose top sits about level with the GPU die, so one cold plate can touch both. The drawing makes each stack about three times taller than that so its DRAM layers can be counted as bands on one cut face; the band spacing and the TSV lines are illustrative, and the memory type printed on each top is a label for the reader, not a copy of a real part marking. On H100 the sixth site is drawn as a blank silicon spacer outlined in dashes, matching five working stacks; its exact construction is not taken from a teardown.' },
  'die-floorplan-drawing': { title: 'GPU die surface and floorplan, as drawn', value: 'illustrative x-ray; not a real floorplan', why: 'The visible top of a flip-chip die is its plain silicon backside, which is how the die is drawn. The line pattern shown in the data and heat layers is an x-ray illustration of the kinds of blocks a GPU die holds (compute clusters, an L2 cache band, memory interfaces along the HBM edges, the die-to-die interface along the seam). NVIDIA publishes no floorplan for these dies, so block count, sizes and positions are not taken from a die photo.' },
  'cowos-bridge-drawing': { title: 'Interposer bridges and microbumps, as drawn', value: 'representative count, size and placement', why: 'TechInsights reports that Blackwell uses the bridge-based CoWoS-L variant instead of a monolithic silicon interposer. How many bridges it holds, their sizes and positions are not public; the drawing puts one long bridge under the die seam and one under each die-to-HBM edge in a dark organic interposer body. The microbump fields under each die and stack, their pitch and the underfill are illustrative.' },
  'package-solder-drawing': { title: 'Solder balls and bumps, as drawn', value: 'fewer, larger and farther apart than real; representative', why: 'The package is drawn with 676 board-side solder balls on a 3 mm pitch and about a thousand bumps under the interposer on a 1.8 mm pitch so that single joints stay visible. Real large server packages use far finer pitches and many more joints; NVIDIA publishes no ball map or pitch for these packages, so counts, pitch and the reflowed ball shape are illustrative.' },
  'thermal-stack-layers': { title: 'Layers between die and coolant', value: 'die → thermal interface → lid → thermal interface → cold plate or heat sink; H100: no lid', why: 'A reseller listing for the H100 SXM5 module describes a bare die with no heat spreader, the heat sink mating directly to the silicon, so on H100 the lifted plate is drawn without a skirt and stands for the heat sink’s flat base. No public source found states whether the Blackwell or Rubin packages carry a lid; they are drawn with a representative one, a flat plate with a straight skirt like the heat spreaders in public package photos, and it is drawn see-through, with an illustrative glow over the dies, so the heat path stays visible. The interface materials, their thickness and the plate’s dimensions are representative; the exact stack is proprietary to each vendor and cooling design.' },
  'hbm-power-share': { title: 'HBM’s share of GPU package power', value: '≈8–15%', why: 'No chipmaker publishes a per-generation split of package power between the compute die and the HBM stacks; this range is the rough order of magnitude used across component-power discussions of GPUs in this class, and it should move with HBM generation and stack count.' },
  'token-byte-size': { title: 'Bytes per output token, text', value: 'a few bytes', why: 'A token ID is 2–4 bytes as the model emits it, or a few characters (≈4 bytes in UTF-8) once decoded to text; either way the network payload per token is tiny next to the compute behind it, which is the point the card makes.' },
  'rubin-packaging': { title: 'Rubin packaging technology', value: 'TSMC CoWoS-L (carried over)', why: 'NVIDIA has not stated Rubin’s packaging technology; this carries forward CoWoS-L, which trade analysis (Tom’s Hardware) reports TSMC uses for Blackwell’s two-die package, pending Rubin’s own teardown.' },
  'rubin-die-area': { title: 'Rubin die area', value: '≈16 cm² total, two dies (carried over)', why: 'NVIDIA has not published Rubin’s die size. The figure shown carries forward the reticle-limited two-die total trade press reported for Blackwell, pending an independent teardown of Rubin.' },
  'rubin-nvhbi-bandwidth': { title: 'Rubin NV-HBI die-to-die bandwidth', value: '10 TB/s (carried over)', why: 'NVIDIA confirms Rubin’s two dies are joined by NV-HBI but has not published its bandwidth. The figure shown carries forward Blackwell’s confirmed 10 TB/s, pending Rubin’s own datasheet.' },
  'rubin-package-power': { title: 'Rubin GPU package power', value: '≈1,800 W', why: 'NVIDIA has not published a per-GPU TDP for Rubin. 1,800 W is the figure shown at NVIDIA’s own early Vera Rubin materials; trade press (Tom’s Hardware) has since reported the shipping figure may run higher, up to 2,300 W, so this is treated as an early, not a final, number.' },
  'hbm-thermal-limit-other-gens': { title: 'HBM operating-temperature limit, other generations', value: '≈105 °C', why: 'Micron publishes 0–105 °C specifically for HBM3E (GB200/GB300’s memory). H100’s HBM3 and Rubin’s HBM4 do not have a confirmed public limit, so the same figure is carried over as a reasonable proxy for stacked DRAM of this class.' },

